# Gate step 1196-5hva — data, not code.
#
# The first fixture fleet-heartbeat.sh has ever had. Hermetic: each arm builds a
# throwaway repo with its own plan/ tree, git history and a STUB plan binary
# injected through TILLANDSIAS_PLAN_BIN, so no arm reads this checkout's ledger
# or the real fleet's state.
#
# WHY A CLASSIFIER'S BUCKET NAMES ARE WORTH A GATE STEP: they are instructions.
# WEDGED means "adjudicate its worktree", BLOCKED means "read the record it
# filed". During 1193-yw6u both macOS hosts read WEDGED while a ledger packet
# named a trunk-wide red another host had already diagnosed, so the advice
# pointed at local dirt that was not there.
#
# THE ARMS THAT MATTER ARE THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS (3, 4, 6, 7). A detector that
# resurrects a lifted block from an immutable fragment, or invents one when it
# cannot fold the ledger, or takes another host's block for this one's, says
# "blocked" about everything — worse than the silence it replaced.
#
# STEP_SCRIPT is a LITERAL path (1063-nraf).
STEP_DESC="Checking a blocker filed in the ledger is named BLOCKED, not WEDGED (1196-5hva)"
STEP_SCRIPT="scripts/test-fleet-heartbeat-sees-a-ledger-filed-blocker.sh"
STEP_ERROR="the fleet heartbeat's blocked detection regressed (1196-5hva) — either a host that filed its blocker reads WEDGED again (whose prescribed response, adjudicate the worktree, points away from a cause the host already named), or the detector has widened and is calling unexplained silence 'blocked'; see the failing arm above"
STEP_OK="fleet-heartbeat ledger-blocker classification check passed"
# Exit 3 means the classifier is absent — nothing was examined, and the fixture
# says so by name rather than passing.
STEP_SKIP_EXIT=3
STEP_SKIP_DESC="scripts/fleet-heartbeat.sh is absent, so the classification could not be exercised"
